I think such a program is indeed useful.

What I find inferior is, a) you haven't provided source code,
b) it's written for a proprietary software platform, c) there
seems no description of the program shipped with the zip file.

Given that it's written for a completely free game, point a)
is an issue of style and and also a security issue. I've added
point b) because what your program does seems quite simple, so
a platform independent version seems not to require too much
effort. And solving point c) would help others to develop a
platform independent version or a functionally compatible
version for other platforms in case that you don't care.

I say all that because I am confident to program a similar tool
in less than 10 lines of shell code. All you have to do[*] is
provide a few files in a config directory, each file named by a
pattern that defines any subset of classes you want to play[**]
and the respective files will contain the necessary rc-settings.
The script would show a menu of the possibilities based on the
files existing in the config directory, and the selection would
load the file and insert/replace the respective lines into the
player's nethack rc-file.[***]

Janis

[*] Given that the functional specification doesn't exceed what
you've written: "predefine your name, class/role, race, alignment,
and a few pickup options".

[**] Say files named like "val-hum-neu-fem.nhrc", "wiz-hum.nhrc".

[***] A variant could be that you just keep multiple complete
rc-files in parallel and just select the one you want to play,
so you'd more data in each file but could avoid the replacement.
(Though the substitution step has advantages.)


